<h2>The three limbs of Rule 41</h2>
<p>Order XXI Rule 41 operates in three steps of escalating force. Rule 41(1) permits the court, on the application of the decree-holder, to orally examine the judgment debtor — or, where the debtor is a corporation, any officer of it — as to whether any debts are owed to the debtor and whether it has any property or means of satisfying the decree. The court may also order production of books and documents in the debtor's possession.</p>
<p>Rule 41(2) is the provision with teeth. Where a decree for payment of money has remained unsatisfied for thirty days, the court shall, on the decree-holder's application and without prejudice to Rule 41(1), require the judgment debtor, or any officer of a corporate debtor, to file an affidavit stating the particulars of its assets. The word is "shall": once the threshold is met, the direction follows as of course.</p>
<p>Rule 41(3) supplies the sanction. Disobedience of an order under Rule 41(2) is punishable, on the executing court's order, with detention in civil prison for a term which may extend to three months, unless the court directs release before its expiry.</p>

<h2>When to deploy it</h2>
<p>Rule 41 earns its keep in two recurring situations. The first is the blind-start execution: the decree-holder dealt with the debtor commercially but never knew its asset position, and cannot give the court the particulars that a warrant of attachment demands. The second is the stalled execution: attachment was attempted against known assets and failed — premises vacated, accounts emptied, stock removed — and the trail has gone cold. In both, the cheapest source of information about the debtor's assets is the debtor, and Rule 41 is the mechanism for tapping it under oath. In commercial court executions, where decrees are often substantial and debtors corporate, an early Rule 41(2) application has become close to standard practice, and executing courts are accustomed to granting it at the first or second hearing.</p>

<h2>Drafting the application</h2>
<p>The application is short but should be exact. It should recite the decree, the amount outstanding with interest computed to date, the steps already taken in execution and their results, and the fact that the decree has remained unsatisfied for over thirty days. Where the debtor is a company, the application should name the officers from whom the affidavit is sought, with their designations, so that the order fixes personal responsibility. It is sound practice to annex a draft format of the affidavit covering the heads of disclosure the decree-holder wants, and to seek a direction that the affidavit follow that format within a fixed time.</p>

<h2>What a compliant affidavit must disclose</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li>Immovable property held in the debtor's name or beneficially, with location, survey or municipal particulars, and encumbrances.</li>
    <li>Bank accounts and deposits, with bank, branch and account particulars, and balances as on the affidavit date.</li>
    <li>Movable assets of value: vehicles, plant and machinery, stock-in-trade, office equipment.</li>
    <li>Receivables and debts owed to the judgment debtor, naming the debtors and amounts.</li>
    <li>Shares, securities, mutual funds and other financial instruments.</li>
    <li>Interests in partnerships, LLPs or other entities, and, for individuals, sources of income.</li>
    <li>Assets transferred or disposed of after the decree, with particulars of transferee and consideration.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>The last head matters. An affidavit confined to what the debtor currently holds tells only half the story; disclosure of post-decree transfers equips the decree-holder to pursue transactions designed to defeat the decree through the remedies the law provides against such transfers.</p>

<h2>Testing an evasive affidavit</h2>
<p>Debtors rarely refuse outright; they file thin affidavits — a single page declaring no assets worth disclosing. The decree-holder's response is to confront the affidavit with independent material: balance sheets and annual returns filed with the Registrar of Companies, GST and income-tax disclosures already in the decree-holder's hands from the commercial relationship, property records, and the debtor's own pleadings in other proceedings. Armed with contradictions, the decree-holder can seek oral examination under Rule 41(1) on the affidavit, a further and better affidavit, and ultimately action under Rule 41(3) on the footing that an affidavit false in material particulars is no compliance at all. Executing courts treat demonstrated evasion seriously, because the entire provision exists to prevent it.</p>

<h2>From disclosure to recovery</h2>
<p>Rule 41 produces information, not money; the follow-on steps convert one into the other. Disclosed immovable and movable property is attached and sold through the ordinary machinery of Order XXI. Disclosed receivables and bank balances are reached through garnishee proceedings, which have their own detailed article on this site. Disclosed shareholdings and partnership interests are attached through the modes the Code provides for such property. The affidavit also serves a quieter function: once a debtor has sworn to its asset position, settlement discussions proceed on a shared factual floor, and instalment proposals can be evaluated against disclosed means rather than pleaded poverty.</p>

<h2>Corporate debtors and responsible officers</h2>
<p>Against companies, the practical craft lies in choosing the deponent. A direction against the company in the abstract invites affidavits sworn by junior employees who profess ignorance. Decree-holders should press for the affidavit of a named director or key managerial officer, and for a direction that the deponent appear for examination if required. The coercive force of Rule 41(3) is felt only when a specific individual faces the prospect of civil prison for non-compliance, and executing courts, properly moved, will frame their orders accordingly.</p>
